      "judgment": {
        "status": "confirmed",
        "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-130848: subtracting three times row 3 from row 1 gives (0,-8,10), leaving the other rows unchanged. q-177648: X_0 is {0}×[0,1] and is contained in every X_i, so the intersection is the closed vertical segment in option 3; the source image depicts the same segment. q-181303: under the source's convention N={1,2,...}, R×N consists of full horizontal lines at positive integer heights. Option 1 and the source diagram illustrate this same infinite family using different numbers of sample lines. q-105152: b−3a_1=(-6,4,8,2)=2a_2, so h=2. q-34600: b−5a_1=(6,0,15,-9)=3a_2, so l=3. q-73937: x=(b−2a)/5=(1,-4,8). q-73936: x=(4a−b)/2=(-2,15,-3). q-73951: the last two component equations force x_1=-2 and x_2=3, but the first component then equals 4 rather than 3; there is no solution. q-73952: (x_1,x_2)=(-3,4) satisfies every component, giving sum 1. q-105168: (x_1,x_2,x_3)=(-7,1,6) satisfies every component, giving sum 0. q-73992: (2,3,-2) satisfies every component, giving sum 3. q-34058: b=2a_1+a_2, so membership holds and the coefficient product is 2. q-34218: b=2a_1+4a_2−2a_3, so membership holds and the product is -16. q-74558: every vector in the span has equal first and third coordinates; k=0 is necessary and sufficient, with coefficients (2,1). q-74560: every vector in the span has third coordinate twice its first; k=-24 is necessary and sufficient, with coefficients (-2,-2). q-105248: the third component forces s=-t, and the first then forces t=-1 and s=1; the second component becomes 6 rather than 5, so u does not lie on the plane. The source's use of x_1 and x_2 in its final contradictory row is a harmless notation slip; its conclusion is correct.",
