        "knowledge_point": "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination",
        "problem": "Find $h$, if $3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $h$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ h[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $h=2.$",
